-- DICT-SEMANTICS §9 "Signature authority", final sentence: "The entailment side
-- condition is VECTOR-VALUED on multi-parameter predicates: `Q_sig = {Ix a b, Ix
-- c d}` does NOT entail an inferred joint `Ix a d` (each ARGUMENT appears in
-- `Q_sig`, but the PREDICATE does not), so that binding is REJECTED rather than
-- widened -- the per-argument reading would be unsound."
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived): the body writes `ix p s`, whose goal is
-- `Ix a d` -- pairing the first variable of one declared predicate with the
-- second variable of the other. `Q_sig ⊩ P'ᵢ` fails, so REJECT.
--
-- OBSERVED: check/run/build all REJECT with `T-MISSING-CONSTRAINT`
-- ("Could not deduce 'Ix a d' from the signature of 'joint'"). CONFORMANT.
--
-- This is the row that separates a genuinely n-ary obligation representation
-- from #607's "two separate single-param facts" one. Under the per-argument
-- reading `a` is covered by `Ix a b` and `d` is covered by `Ix c d`, so the
-- broken representation ACCEPTS -- and then `ix p s` dispatches on a predicate
-- no caller ever discharged.
interface Ix a i where
  ix : a -> i -> Int

impl Ix Int Int where
  ix a i = a + i

joint : (Ix a b, Ix c d) => a -> b -> c -> d -> Int
joint p q r s = ix p s

main = println (joint (1 : Int) (2 : Int) (3 : Int) (4 : Int))
